Why This Is Asked
- Tests dynamic augmentation of a trie with aggregated metrics and tie-breaking logic.

Key Concepts
- Trie augmentation with subtreeSum and subtreeBest; O(length) updates; O(length) queries.
- Correct handling of weight deltas, insertions, and removals; lexicographic tiebreaks.

Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.subtreeSum = 0;
    this.bestWord = null;
    this.bestWeight = 0;
  }
}
class DynamicDict {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.wordWeights = new Map();
  }
  add(word, delta) {
    let node = this.root;
    const newWeight = (this.wordWeights.get(word) || 0) + delta;
    this.wordWeights.set(word, newWeight);
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
      node.subtreeSum += delta;
      if (newWeight > node.bestWeight || 
          (newWeight === node.bestWeight && word < node.bestWord)) {
        node.bestWord = word;
        node.bestWeight = newWeight;
      }
    }
  }
  query(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        return [0, null];
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    return [node.subtreeSum, node.bestWord];
  }
}
```

Complexity Analysis
- `add(word, delta)`: O(|word|) time, O(1) extra space per character
- `query(prefix)`: O(|prefix|) time, O(1) extra space

Edge Cases
- Negative deltas potentially removing words completely
- Multiple words with equal maximum weights requiring lexicographic tie-breaking
- Empty queries and non-existent prefixes
- Words sharing common prefixes affecting aggregate calculations

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates ability to implement non-recursive mergesort on a linked list, a common real-world pattern where recursion is costly or prohibited. Tests pointer manipulation, stability of merge, and correctness of bottom-up passes, plus O(n log n) time and O(1) extra space.

## Key Concepts

- Bottom-up mergesort
- In-place linked-list merge
- Stability
- Time and space complexity

## Code Example

```javascript
function mergeTwo(l1, l2) {
  const dummy = { val: 0, next: null };
  let tail = dummy;
  while (l1 && l2) {
    if (l1.val <= l2.val) {
      tail.next = l1;
      l1 = l1.next;
    } else {
      tail.next = l2;
      l2 = l2.next;
    }
    tail = tail.next;
  }
  tail.next = l1 || l2;
  return dummy.next;
}

function sortList(head) {
  if (!head || !head.next) return head;
  // length
  let len = 0, p = head;
  while (p) { len++; p = p.next; }
  const dummy = { next: head };
  for (let w = 1; w < len; w <<= 1) {
    let prev = dummy;
    let curr = dummy.next;
    while (curr) {
      // split left
      let left = curr;
      let i = 1;
      while (i < w && curr && curr.next) { curr = curr.next; i++; }
      const right = curr.next;
      curr.next = null;
      // split right
      curr = right;
      i = 1;
      while (i < w && curr && curr.next) { curr = curr.next; i++; }
      const next = curr ? curr.next : null;
      if (curr) curr.next = null;
      const merged = mergeTwo(left, right);
      prev.next = merged;
      while (prev.next) prev = prev.next;
      curr = next;
    }
  }
  return dummy.next;
}
```

